# Tooltip — a term with its explanation attached: the label reads inline, the tip
# appears over it on hover. Useful wherever a word has to be short and still be
# understood ("Reposts", "Coordinate", "@optimistic").
#
# `Tooltip`'s trigger is TEXT. That used to be the end of the story, and the note
# that said so — "a component cannot accept child nodes, so a tooltip that wraps
# whatever you put inside it cannot be written here at all" — has been stale since
# `slot` shipped. It can be written, and `TooltipOn` is it:
#
#     use Tooltip("Reposts", "Someone shared this with their own followers.")
#
#     use TooltipOn("Signed by the authority, not by the client."):
#         use Tag("verified", "ok")
#
# `Tooltip` stays for the common case — a word with a gloss is one node, and a
# component call with a slot for one word is three lines where one would do — and
# `TooltipOn` takes the trigger as children, so an icon, a badge, a stat or a whole
# row can carry a tip. Neither is built on the other: `Tooltip` would have to pass
# a parameter into `TooltipOn`'s slot, and a slot child is evaluated in the
# CALLEE's scope, so the name would resolve against `TooltipOn`'s own parameters
# first. They share the class names and the stylesheet instead, which is the part
# that actually has to stay in step.
#
# ONE THING TO KNOW BEFORE PUTTING A PARAMETER IN `TooltipOn`'S SLOT, and it is a
# compiler bug rather than a design of this facet: slot children resolve locals
# against the callee first. A child that interpolates a caller parameter or a
# `for` variable named `tip` will silently render THIS component's tip instead.
# Name it something else, or pass the value through a component of your own.
#
# Reveal is hover-only. The language has no way to put `tabindex` or an `aria-*`
# attribute on a node, so the trigger cannot be focused and the tip cannot be
# announced; it is a convenience, never the only place a fact is written down.
app FacetUI:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tip { position: relative; display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
                border-bottom: 1px dotted var(--fa-muted); cursor: help; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tip-body { position: absolute; bottom: calc(100% + 8px); left: 50%;
                     transform: translateX(-50%); width: max-content; max-width: 260px;
                     padding: 8px 10px; border-radius: 8px; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.35;
                     background: var(--x-panel, #16181c); color: var(--fa-fg);
                     border: 1px solid var(--x-line, #2f3336);
                     box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(0,0,0,.4);
                     opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; transition: opacity .12s ease; z-index: 70; }
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tip:hover .x-tip-body { opacity: 1; visibility: visible; }
        # The slot-bearing variant. No underline and no `cursor: help`, because the
        # trigger is a node that already has its own affordance; the tip is an
        # addition to it rather than a replacement for it.
        [data-fa-mount] .x-tip-plain { border-bottom: none; cursor: inherit; gap: 0;
                        padding: 0; background: none; }
    component Tooltip(label: text, tip: text):
        row class "x-tip":
            text "{label}"
            text "{tip}" class "x-tip-body"
    # The same tip, over whatever the caller puts inside it. The dotted underline
    # is dropped here: it is right under a word and wrong under an icon or a
    # badge, and a caller who wants it back adds `.x-tip` styling of their own.
    component TooltipOn(tip: text):
        row class "x-tip x-tip-plain":
            slot
            text "{tip}" class "x-tip-body"
